  "full_response": "To create a sample Postman collection which you can use for export that includes environment variables with API keys, OAuth tokens, and basic auth credentials, follow these steps:\n\n1. First, make sure you have Postman installed and set up on your computer.\n\n2. Open Postman and create a new file by clicking on \"New > Collection\". Name your collection appropriately, e.g. \"API Integration Sample\".\n\n3. Create a new request file within the collection you just created by clicking on \"Export > Export Collection\". Save the collection to your desired location when.\n\n4. For each API endpoint within your collection, you will need to set up your environment variables. Click on your collection and edit the requests you want to add environment variables.\n\n5. Go to the \"Environment\" menu at the top and create a new environment, e.g., \"prod\".\n\n6. Add environment variables to your newly created environment. The following schema includes the typical values for API keys, OAuth tokens, and basic auth credentials:\n\n   - `API_KEY`: Your custom API key\n   - `OAUTH_TOKEN`: The OAuth token you've received from the service provider for user authentication\n   - `OAUTH_TOKEN_SECRET`: The corresponding secret token for OAuth\n   - `BASIC_AUTH_USER`: The username for Basic Auth\n   - `BASIC_AUTH_PASS`: The password for Basic Auth\n\n7. Save the environment using the \"Add\" button and ensure you choose the \"Current Values\" option.\n\n8. Now, go to each API request and set up the respective environment variables in the \"Pre-request Scripts\" or \"Tests\" tab.\n\nBelow is an example of a request setup with multiple environment variables:\n\n   - Request URL: `https://api.example.com/data`\n   - Method: `GET`\n   - Headers: Add the `x-api-key: {{API_KEY}}` header to include the API key.\n\nFor OAuth:\n   - Authentication type: \"OAuth2\"\n   - Access Token URL: The token endpoint provided by the API\n   - Scope: Define the scope access, if needed\n   - Bearer Token: `{{OAUTH_TOKEN}}`\n\nFor Basic Authentication:\n   - Type: \"Basic Auth\"\n   - Username: `{{BASIC_AUTH_USER}}`\n   - Password: `{{BASIC_AUTH_PASS}}`\n\nOnce you have populated your individual requests with the appropriate environment variables, you can then export your collection as previously instructed.\n\n9. Finally, export your collection again after setting up all of your endpoints, so",
